# MetadataTeam-specific rules — `report_metadata`

These rules encode lessons from the Phase 1 validation set
(`ground_truth/demo.json` + `ground_truth/phase1/*.json`). Treat them as
hard requirements that override generic intuitions.

## 1. Fax-header noise must be ignored

Some reports arrive by fax. The preprocessor strips the fax-transport band
before you see the text, but echo signatures of the band (timestamps in the
form `MM.DD.YYYY HH:MM:SS Page: N of M Sender: <lab> Recipient: <fax number>`)
may still appear faintly. Specifically:

- **Never extract a date from a fax-header timestamp.** The Block Profiler
  has tagged any surviving such block as `text_role: fax_transport_noise`.
  Inspect `state_read('block_profiles')` if a date candidate's page-1
  location overlaps the top fax band — that's the fax timestamp, not the
  Report_Date.
- The legitimate `Collection_Date`, `Received_Date`, and `Report_Date` are
  always in the report body's metadata block (usually right-column on
  page 1), labelled explicitly.

## 2. Vendor inference rule (`Vendor_Name`)

Order of preference:

1. **Explicit vendor wordmark or address** — masthead logo (e.g.
   "NeoGenomics", "Tempus", "Foundation Medicine"), copyright footer
   (e.g. "© YYYY NeoGenomics Laboratories, Inc."), lab address with the
   vendor's name. Use the canonical vendor name as printed.
2. **Product marker inference** — if no explicit wordmark, infer the
   vendor from any distinctive product / assay / test-line wordmark or
   trademarked identifier that the report prints. The principle: a
   wordmark that uniquely identifies a single commercial testing lab
   maps to that lab. Examples (illustrative, NOT a filter — apply the
   principle to any wordmark you recognize as a single-vendor product
   line): `"xT"` / `"xT genomic profiling"` → Tempus; `"FoundationOne"`
   / `"F1CDx"` → Foundation Medicine; `"xGEN"` → Caris; `"Guardant"`
   → Guardant Health; `"MyChoice"` → Myriad Genetics.
3. **Neither present** → `null`. Do not guess from clinic name.

`Vendor_Name` has `extraction_mode: VERBATIM_OR_INFERRED` — inference
is allowed via the product-marker principle in step 2, not just the
listed examples. If you map a wordmark not in the example list, set
`needs_review: true` with a `review_reason` naming the wordmark you
used so an SME can confirm.

## 3. Practice vs vendor disambiguation — critical

The **practice** is the *ordering clinic* (Practice_*). The **vendor** is
the *testing lab* (Vendor_Name). These are different entities even when
both appear in the report. Examples:

- `demo.pdf`: vendor = `NeoGenomics`, practice = `Hematology & Oncology Consultants` at 28078 Baxter Rd. Suite 140, Murrieta, CA 92563.
- `doc_3.pdf` (Tempus xT): vendor = `Tempus` (from "xT" marker), practice = `Doyle PLC` (the institution beneath "Physician" in the left margin).

The practice's address never belongs to `Vendor_Name`. The vendor's
copyright footer never belongs to `Practice_Name`.

## 4. Physician location varies by template

Check **all three** locations before emitting `null`:

1. **Right-column metadata block** (`demo.pdf` pattern) — labels like
   "Ordering Physician(s):" or "Treating Physician(s):".
2. **Left-margin "Physician" label** (`doc_*` Tempus xT pattern).
3. **Page footer** (`doc2_*` unknown-vendor pattern) — every page footer
   reads `PATIENT: <name>  CASE NUMBER: <#>  PHYSICIAN: Dr. <name>`.
   The `"Ordered By"` section header on page 1 of `doc2_*` reports has
   no value beneath it; do NOT emit null without checking the footer.

If the report shows separate Ordering and Treating physicians, prefer
**Ordering**. If they're the same person, that's normal.

## 5. Title parsing for `Ordering_Provider_*`

Parse the provider name regardless of its surface form. The same name
can appear with any combination of these shape attributes — handle them
all:

- **Title placement** — either as a prefix (`Dr. <name>`) OR as a
  comma-suffix (`<name>, <title>`).
- **Title vocabulary** — open-ended medical title strings; emit whatever
  surface the report uses VERBATIM in `Ordering_Provider_Title`. Common
  shapes: `Dr.`, `M.D.`, `D.O.`, `PA-C`, but do NOT treat this as a
  closed list.
- **Casing** — the source may use ALL CAPS, Title Case, or mixed; treat
  casing as a presentation detail, not a parsing signal.
- **Middle initials / middle names** — may be embedded between first
  and last; drop them (no schema field) instead of stuffing into either
  name field.

Examples (illustrative, NOT exhaustive):
`STANLEY D SCHINKE, M.D.` · `Dr. Jennifer Walker` ·
`Wanda Nguyen, M.D.` · `Jorge Thomas, D.O.`

Extract as:
- **`Ordering_Provider_First_Name`** — proper case (e.g. "Stanley", "Jennifer"). Cast all-caps to Title Case for proper-name fields *only* — this is the one exception to the VERBATIM rule. Why: downstream consumers (CRM, EHR) require Title Case.
- **`Ordering_Provider_Last_Name`** — proper case.
- **`Ordering_Provider_Title`** — VERBATIM as printed: `M.D.`, `D.O.`, `PA-C`, `Dr.`, etc. Preserve periods.
- Middle initials and middle names are **dropped** (no schema field). Do not stuff them into first or last name.

## 6. Strict MRN labeling (`Patient_MRN`)

`Patient_MRN` is **null** unless the source contains a literal label that
is exactly one of:
- `MRN`
- `Medical Record #`
- `Med Rec #`

Do **NOT** treat as MRN any of:
- `Accession Number`, `Case Number`, `CaseNo`, `Case No`
- `ID #`, `Patient ID`, `Specimen ID`
- `Order #`

In the Phase 1 corpus, **every** ground-truth file has `Patient_MRN: null`.
A blank `MRN:` field (label present, value missing) is also `null`.

## 7. ISO date normalization

`Collection_Date`, `Received_Date`, `Report_Date`, `Patient_DOB` are all
`extraction_mode: DERIVED`. Use the `date_parser` tool to convert any
source format to ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`. Drop time-of-day and timezone.

Source examples that must all normalize correctly:
- `08/12/2025 01:00:00 PM` → `2025-08-12`
- `08/13/2025 01:15:00 PM PDT` → `2025-08-13`
- `08/26/2025 07:41:01 PM ET` → `2025-08-26`
- `01/25/1941 / M` → `1941-01-25` (drop the trailing sex field)
- Already-ISO `2024-03-23` → unchanged

If the source has no date at all in a slot (e.g. `doc2_*` reports omit
`Report_Date`), emit `null`.

## 8. Practice address parsing

The address may be printed on 2-4 lines:
```
28078 Baxter Rd. Suite 140
Murrieta, CA 92563
```

Map to:
- `Practice_Street_Address_1` = `"28078 Baxter Rd."` (drop suite from line 1)
- `Practice_Street_Address_2` = `"Suite 140"` (suite/unit/floor goes here)
- `Practice_City` = `"Murrieta"`
- `Practice_State` = `"CA"` (two-letter, uppercase)
- `Practice_ZIP_5digit` = `"92563"`
- `Practice_ZIP_4digit` = `null` (or the 4-digit suffix when present as ZIP+4)

If the line has only the street with no suite, leave
`Practice_Street_Address_2` as `null` rather than empty string.

## 9. NPI validation

If you extract a 10-digit NPI candidate (rare in the Phase 1 corpus — most
ground truths have `null`), call `npi_validator` before emitting. Emit
`null` on failure rather than the unvalidated string.

## 10. `Test_Name` vs `Procedure`

- `Test_Name` is the named test or panel as printed:
  `"JAK2 V617F Mutation Analysis - Quantitative"`, `"xT genomic profiling"`,
  `"Molecular profiling (multi-biomarker panel)"`.
- `Procedure` is the methodology, only emit if the source literally says it:
  `"Quantitative PCR"`, `"Next Generation Sequencing"`, `"FISH"`, `"IHC"`,
  `"Sanger sequencing"`.
- **`Test_Name` is a short label, never a results dump.** It is a single
  named test/panel/examination title (typically ≤ ~8 words), not a
  concatenation of result lines, findings, or a whole section body. Never fold
  ancillary-study lines, biomarker results, or multi-line content into it — if
  you find yourself joining lines with `|` or newlines, you've captured a result
  body, not a name. When no explicit assay name is printed (common on surgical
  pathology reports), use the examination/report type itself (e.g.
  `"Surgical Pathology"`) — the same value the masthead/report_title conveys.

For `demo.pdf`, the Methodology paragraph explicitly says "quantitative PCR
and probe analysis" — that's a `Procedure` of `"Quantitative PCR"`. For
`doc_*` (Tempus xT), the methodology phrase isn't printed verbatim — emit
`null` for `Procedure`.

## 11. `report_title` and `total_pages`

- `report_title` is VERBATIM — the masthead section header that names what
  this report is, e.g. `"Molecular Genetics"`, `"Final Report"`,
  `"Colorectal Sample"`. Not the test name (that's `Test_Name`).
- `total_pages` is DERIVED — the integer page count, not a string. Pull
  from the PDF's actual page count via `state_read('doc_profile')`.

## 12. `llm_confidence_score`

Per the base instructions, emit a top-level float in [0.0, 1.0]. Calibrate:
- 0.95+ — every required field grounded in clear, unambiguous source text.
- 0.85-0.95 — minor ambiguity in 1-2 fields (e.g. you had to choose between
  two physician names printed in the same block).
- 0.70-0.85 — significant inference required (vendor inferred from product
  marker, address split across non-adjacent fragments).
- <0.70 — you are guessing on a required field. The decision router will
  flag the doc to SME at <0.85.

## 13. Provenance contract — REQUIRED

For every field you emit in `report_metadata`, you MUST also emit a
corresponding entry in `report_metadata.provenance` recording **where the
value came from**. This is non-optional and powers the UI's block-centric
explorer. Without provenance, the SME reviewer cannot verify your work.

The `provenance` is an **ARRAY** — ONE entry per field you populated, each entry
carrying its own `field_name`. Use an ARRAY (not a keyed object):

```json
"provenance": [
  { "field_name": "Patient_First_Name", "block_id": "b5", "page": 1,
    "type": "verbatim" },
  { "field_name": "Vendor_Name", "block_id": "b2", "page": 1, "type": "inferred",
    "rationale": "Inferred from 'xT' product marker per vendor inference rule" },
  { "field_name": "Patient_MRN", "block_id": null, "page": null, "type": "absent" },
  { "field_name": "Collection_Date", "block_id": "b8", "page": 1, "type": "derived",
    "rationale": "Normalized '08/12/2025 01:00:00 PM' → '2025-08-12'" }
]
```

### `type` values — pick exactly one per field

- **`verbatim`** — value appears literally in the source. `block_id` MUST
  cite a DocAI block whose text contains the value (or a normalized form
  of it for proper-name fields). Use for: `Patient_First_Name`,
  `Patient_Last_Name`, `Practice_*`, `Ordering_Provider_First_Name`,
  `Ordering_Provider_Last_Name`, `Ordering_Provider_Title`,
  `Test_Name`, `Procedure`, `report_title`, when those are stated outright.

- **`inferred`** — value derived from documented signals (Rules 2, 4, 5).
  `block_id` cites the block containing the SIGNAL (e.g. the `xT` product
  marker), not a block containing the inferred value (which won't exist).
  Include a `rationale` naming which rule fired.

- **`derived`** — value was computed or normalized from a source. Use for
  dates normalized to ISO YYYY-MM-DD (Rule 7), `total_pages` (computed from
  the document), `Practice_State` (uppercased), proper-case name fields
  (Rule 5 — `STANLEY` → `Stanley`). Cite the source block + rationale.

- **`absent`** — value is null because the source has no value at this
  field. `block_id` and `page` are null. Most `null` outputs use this.
  Examples: most `Patient_MRN` (strict-MRN rule), most NPI fields, ZIP+4.

### How to get block_ids

Every tool result you receive contains `block_id` references:
- `pdf_page_loader` returns a list of `blocks` for that page; each has `block_id`.
- `pdf_text_search` returns matches with `block_id`.
- `docai_layout_lookup` returns blocks with `block_id`.
- `state_read('block_profiles')` returns the full classification with `block_id`s.

**Cite only block_ids that appeared in tool results.** Fabricated block_ids
will fail downstream validation.

### Special case: `llm_confidence_score`

This field is your own self-report, not extracted from source. Emit its
provenance as an array entry:
```json
{ "field_name": "llm_confidence_score", "block_id": null, "page": null,
  "type": "derived", "rationale": "Model self-confidence; not extracted from source" }
```

## 14. Production-parity fields (Report_Type, Accession_Number, Signing_Pathologist, provider phone / additional)

These mirror the production schema's `administrative_info`. All VERBATIM; emit
`null` when not present.

- `Report_Type` — the report's type/category as printed (e.g. "Surgical
  Pathology", "Molecular", "Cytology", "Hematopathology"). Distinct from
  `report_title` (the masthead) and `Test_Name` (the assay). For
  `demo.pdf` (a "Molecular Genetics" masthead) there is no separate type label →
  `null`.
- `Accession_Number` — the Accession / Case Number as printed
  (e.g. "7220225/MOL25-157872"). This is **NOT** `Patient_MRN` (keep the strict
  MRN rule). If the report shows "Accession/CaseNo: X", emit X here.
- `Signing_Pathologist` — the pathologist who signed out / electronically
  reviewed the report (e.g. an "Electronic Signature / Reviewed by:" block →
  "James Liang, CGMBS, MB(ASCP)CM"). Not the ordering physician.
- `Ordering_Provider_Phone` — the ordering provider's OWN phone if printed
  separately; do NOT copy the practice/clinic phone here → `null` if only the
  practice phone exists.
- `Additional_Provider_Name` — a second provider the report lists beyond the
  ordering provider (e.g. a "Treating Physician(s):" different from the ordering
  one, or a copy-to). If ordering and treating are the SAME person, emit `null`.

